--- body ---

## Context

The author closes git-erg's Dyne design and implementation work in favor of
documenting the harness's existing Git-notes review workflow. Git notes are
the review-evidence store; a separate Dyne binary is not commissioned.
The reassessment is in git-erg `docs/dyne-review-contract.md` (PR #377).

## Deliverables

- A technical note for HAL describing the problem, the implemented approach,
  evidence and limits: request, review and gate records; identity across
  rebases; retained heads; multi-writer synchronization; transport, recovery,
  and the boundary between Git, the harness and a forge.
- A practical blog post with a reproducible end-to-end example: create and
  review a request, publish and retrieve notes and retained heads from another
  clone, and handle a new revision. Clearly identify commands supplied by Git
  and those supplied by the harness. Verify examples against actual behavior.

## Exit criteria

- [ ] Draft both deliverables and make their intended audiences and relationship clear.
- [ ] Compare relevant prior art, including git-appraise, without an unsupported novelty claim.
- [ ] Demonstrate the documented workflow and report limitations and prerequisites,
      including note transport, concurrent writers, rebase, and the absence of a
      standalone forge interface, CI runner, or access-control guarantee.
- [ ] Provide the drafts and reproducible example for author review before any
      deposit or publication; no outward deposit is authorized by this ticket alone.


## Listing capability and limits (ticket 1450)

The none backend can list the latest native request of each review from a strict
local notes snapshot; state distinguishes open, merged by pinned-head ancestry,
and explicit request abandonment. `number` is an opaque review ID. Listing does
not fetch, numeric PR-adopted reviews are excluded, and unreadable evidence is
never an empty answer. `mergedAt` remains null when ancestry alone is available.
The full dashboard still requires origin and origin/main before reaching its
listing step, so remote-less dashboard operation is not implemented by 1450.
See `conception/2026-10-27-notes-review-spec.md` § Local request listing and
abandonment for the commands, manual read route, state precedence, and exit codes.
